gm-method lessons from a multi-symptom WebGL game debug (backdrop occlusion + player visibility + perf, deployed site):

(1) DEPLOYED-vs-LANDING URL: a github-pages root index.html can be a LANDING page, not the app - the game lived at demo.html. Witness the ACTUAL game URL (check the landing's links + the gh-pages branch file list) before concluding 'app broken on deploy'. Confirm deploy currency via `gh run list --workflow <deploy> --limit N` (success AFTER the fix commits) + a live window.* probe, not assumption.

(2) HEADLESS WITNESS CANNOT REACH GAMEPLAY behind a click-to-play / pointer-lock gate: the witness browser sat at the pre-play overlay, so its screenshots showed a non-gameplay camera state -> 'player invisible' was a witness artifact, NOT a bug (user confirmed visible in real play). LESSON: a 'X missing/broken' report that only reproduces in your headless witness, where the witness can't enter the real interactive state, is suspect - confirm against real play before chasing a render bug. Bugs that need continuous movement / death / pointer-lock to repro are blockedBy:external when the only witness can't reach that state.

(3) BACKDROP DEPTH-SHARE: copying a raymarched-backdrop's depth into the consumer (three) canvas depth buffer to occlude scene geometry FAILS three ways, each witnessed: (a) blitFramebuffer DEPTH from a single-sample FBO into an MSAA default framebuffer = GL_INVALID_OPERATION (witness: hook gl.blitFramebuffer, read getError after - 1282); use a shader gl_FragDepth pass instead (MSAA-safe). (b) Even the shader pass occludes EVERYTHING because the backdrop's depth is NOT projection-comparable: measured by rendering the depth texture to a readable RGBA8 target - a raymarched planet wrote sky=0.655 (should be ~1.0 far), foreground=1.0 (should be near), i.e. a custom/log/inverted encoding, vs three's 0.99 at 50m. Matching near/far is necessary but NOT sufficient; the depth ENCODING must match. (c) The right fix is the backdrop SDK exposing LINEAR eye-distance depth + the consumer re-encoding to its own projection NDC - a real GPU task, not a quick blit. When that's not reachable, default the occlusion OFF (revert when a change regresses - it was vanishing the player) over shipping broken occlusion.

(4) SKINNEDMESH FALSE-CULL: a SkinnedMesh frustum-culls against its BIND-pose bounding sphere transformed by the mesh matrix - it does NOT track the live skinned/scaled/animated pose, so an animated+upscaled avatar VANISHES when its origin nears a frustum edge ('disappears in some spots'). Fix: frustumCulled=false on always-near-camera single skinned meshes (free); never trust the bind sphere for cull on animated meshes. Witness: with the camera aimed so the mesh origin is at the frustum edge, the mesh still contributes a draw call.

(5) PROFILE THE COLD-BOOT vs STEADY-STATE distinction: a user '8s/frame, 90% in rAF' profile was the COLD-BOOT window (shader compile + heightfield build), not steady-state - a timeline sample (fps per 3s) showed 1-15fps for ~24s then 48-61fps steady. Removing the suspect node (planet renderPlanet: 1->7fps) localized the GPU cost. Don't fix steady-state when the complaint is the boot transient.